art of uk governments to  gerrymander the rules  only  politicians fearing the democratic  outcome it would ever contemplate that  let's have the debate on substance where  i and my colleagues will make the case  for independence for scotland becoming  like other independent countries across  the world able to navigate our own  future and let people like liz trust  make a case for the union but then  democratically let the people of  scotland decide that's how democracy  works it's how democracy should always  work um you at the beginning of the  interview you were talking about your  relationship with other uh previous  prime ministers i just want to touch  upon your personal relationship with liz  trust because obviously personal  relationships do matter  in politics and i think i can just play  you what liz trust said  at a hustings in august about you when  she was asked let's have a listen  i feel like i'm a child of the union  that i really believe we are a family  and we're better together and i think  the best thing to do with nicola  sturgeon is ignore her she's an  attention seeker  that's what she is  she's an attention seeker that's what  she is  are you really going to be able to work  with los tras  i will do my best and it is up to his  trust to decide whether she wants to to  work with me let's put the personal  insults to one side again coming back to  the argument i made about if you're  confident in your case you don't need to  try to block democracy or gerrymandered  democracy uh similarly i'd say  just to come in and because it doesn't  make sense i do think  i do feel it is slight a two-way thing  with you guys i have to say you recently  uh said that the main thing liz trust  wanted to talk to you about uh when she  met you  uh i think um  quite recently uh was how she could get  into vogue i've just been interviewed by  vogue who said that was the main thing  she wanted to talk to me about  i remember it because we were there at  the world's biggest climate change  conference in glasgow and that was the  main topic of conversation she was  interested in pursuing just talk me  through what happened there  i was asked a question if i'd had a  conversation with liz trust it's the  only  uh  lengthy conversation or meaningful  conversation i can recall having with  list trust so i i answered the question  about what we talked about and look i'm  perfectly willing to put not to put our  political differences aside because in a  democracy you have political debates but  i will as i did with david cameron  theresa may to some extent boris johnson  i think most people would recognize that  was more difficult to build a working  relationship  i'm not perfect i'm not saying that  i'm blameless when those relationships  go through difficulties but i will try  to build that relationship but the point  i was making is that her comment about  ignoring me is in a similar vein to  trying to block democracy you don't make  a problem that you fear you can't solve  properly go away by simply ignoring it  you engage in attention then debate and  i'm a politician i mean part of your job  as a politician is to get attention for  yourself your policies your values i  mean i don't mean this pejoratively but  i've never looked at let's trust and  think thought that she was  some kind of shrinking violet and that's  not a criticism in politics that's part  of the job that you you have to do but  the ignoring point is perhaps more  fundamental because look people and some  people in scotland vote for me and my  party some people don't some people like  me some people loathe me that's  democracy but i am the democratically  elected first minister of my country and  that means quite a lot of people have  voted for me and when they hear liz  trust saying i should be ignored you  know what they hear is that scotland's  voting choices should be ignored or that  scotland should be ignored and i'll tell  you something i think people in scotland  you know are really getting increasingly  fed up with tory prime ministers that  remember we don't vote for and wouldn't  vote for if we'd had the chance with  less trust trying to ignore and talk  down scotland and that is one of the  many reasons why i think increasing  numbers of people in scotland will  support scotland being an independent  country so that that important  relationship with the rest of the uk is  on the basis of equality  okay now we are out of time but there's  just one thing i would like to ask you  it's something that i've spoken to you  about uh before i know it's something  that you feel uh strongly about um this  week my colleagues liz bakes and agnes  chamber have been looking into  allegations of sexual misconduct and the  culture  in westminster and really this crosses  across all different parties what  they've been looking into  one former snp staff member told them  the attitude in westminster is if it's  not rape it's okay  of course there have been allegations  about members of your own party the  commons watchdog found patrick grody  sexually harassed her junior member of  staff he of course has since quit the  snp  are you happy with the way that your  party has dealt with these allegations  of sexual misconduct  um no i think we can be better and i'm  not going to shy away from that i think  every party every organisation dealing  belatedly i think because too often in  the past these are things that have been  swept under the carpet i think we've all  got lessons to learn i said in the  context of the the patrick grady case  that we had lessons to learn and that is  the case so we are looking at our  procedures as a party as parliamentary  groups all of the time we've gone  through a very painful process as the  scottish government and because of  issues you and i have spoken about  previously and some of the the scrutiny  and the pressure i've had over the past  few years on these issues and in  particular the highest profile one of  these is because i've not been prepared  simply to sweep these things under the  carpet so no i don't think we are as  good as we need to do and that's  something i take seriously and i'm  intending to ensure that we do get  better so that women or men who feel  that they have been  the victim of sexual harassment or  sexual abuse feel confident in coming  forward and feel confident in the  processes that they then use  okay thank you very much nicola sturgeon  their first minister  of scotland
 

